; A data WASM with no `gen` export is read at COMPILE time: the emitter
; allocates `l` bytes, zeroes them, and ships the result in the payload. `l` is
; whatever non-negative i32 the FILE declares, and nothing held it to anything
; — the 57-byte companion here declares 2 GiB and took 6.4 GB of resident
; memory before dying in the PNG writer (§437).
;
; The budget it answers to is the shape generators', which asks the same
; question: how many bytes one compile may materialise for a payload. The `gen`
; shape is deliberately NOT bounded by it — those bytes are computed at runtime
; into a GPU buffer, so a large one is an ordinary request rather than a
; compile-time allocation. examples/test_data_gen.sjon is that positive.
(buffer :name big :file "./oversized_l_data.wasm" :usage [storage copy-dst])
